--TEST--
Each `make` that `Core\Program::constructors<T, C>()` answers is an ordinary closure: it satisfies `is C`, an `int` it is passed widens into a `float` constructor parameter as at a written `new`, and a call through bare `callable` reaches it, `rule:programs/constructors`
--FILE--
<?nvs
interface Gauge {
    public function read(): string;
}

class Meter implements Gauge {
    public function constructor(public float $value) {}
    public function read(): string { return "meter " . $this->value; }
}

class Dial implements Gauge {
    public function constructor(public float $value, public string $unit = "kg") {}
    public function read(): string { return "dial " . $this->value . " " . $this->unit; }
}

array<{class: string, make: callable(int): Gauge}> $rows =
    Core\Program::constructors<Gauge, callable(int): Gauge>();
foreach ($rows as {class: string, make: callable(int): Gauge} $row) {
    mixed $make = $row->make;
    // The closure has the type the call names.
    echo $make is callable(int): Gauge ? "typed" : "untyped", " ";
    echo $make is callable(string): Gauge ? "takes text" : "takes no text", "\n";
}
// The `int` widens into the constructor's `float` parameter.
foreach ($rows as {class: string, make: callable(int): Gauge} $row) {
    echo ($row->make)(3)->read(), "\n";
}
// A call through bare `callable` checks the argument while the program runs.
callable $any = $rows[0]->make;
echo $any(7)->read(), "\n";
--EXPECT--
typed takes no text
typed takes no text
dial 3 kg
meter 3
dial 7 kg
